  "prompt": "Find the common rule that maps an input grid to an output grid, given the examples below. Generate a python script that takes a grid as input and transforms it according to the this rule. Anticipate that this program will be applied to several grids, not only the shown test grid.\n\nYour script must define exactly one callable entrypoint:\ndef solve(grid):\nwhere `grid` is passed as a list of lists of integers (list[list[int]]) and the function returns the transformed grid in the same format.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput:\n8 9 8\n9 8 8\n8 8 8\n2 2 1\n2 2 1\n1 1 2\n4 4 4\n4 4 3\n3 3 3\nOutput:\n4 4 4\n4 4 3\n3 3 3\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:\n1 5 5\n5 1 1\n5 1 1\n3 3 3\n3 6 3\n3 6 6\n7 7 7\n7 2 2\n7 2 2\nOutput:\n3 3 3\n3 6 3\n3 6 6\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput:\n2 2 2\n2 2 3\n2 3 3\n5 7 7\n7 5 5\n7 5 5\n8 8 1\n1 8 1\n1 8 1\nOutput:\n8 8 1\n1 8 1\n1 8 1\n\nExample 4:\n\nInput:\n8 8 4\n4 4 4\n4 4 8\n1 1 3\n1 3 3\n3 3 1\n6 2 2\n2 2 2\n2 2 6\nOutput:\n8 8 4\n4 4 4\n4 4 8\n\nBelow is a test input grid.\n\nInput:\n5 4 4\n4 5 4\n4 5 4\n3 3 2\n3 3 2\n2 2 3\n1 1 1\n1 8 8\n1 8 8\n\nYour final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    # grid is a list of rows, each row is a list of ints (length 3)\n    # The input always consists of three 3\u2011row blocks (total 9 rows).\n    # For each block we compute the sum of its anti\u2011diagonal (top\u2011right to bottom\u2011left).\n    # The block whose anti\u2011diagonal sum is the median of the three sums is returned.\n    block_size = 3\n    num_blocks = len(grid) // block_size\n    blocks = []\n    for i in range(num_blocks):\n        block = grid[i*block_size : (i+1)*block_size]\n        anti_sum = 0\n        for r in range(block_size):\n            anti_sum += block[r][block_size - 1 - r]   # column: 2\u2011r\n        blocks.append((anti_sum, block))\n    # sort by anti\u2011diagonal sum\n    blocks.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\n    # median index (odd number of blocks)\n    median_idx = num_blocks // 2\n    return blocks[median_idx][1]",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    # grid is a list of rows, each row is a list of ints (length 3)\n    # The input always consists of three 3\u2011row blocks (total 9 rows).\n    # For each block we compute the sum of its anti\u2011diagonal (top\u2011right to bottom\u2011left).\n    # The block whose anti\u2011diagonal sum is the median of the three sums is returned.\n    block_size = 3\n    num_blocks = len(grid) // block_size\n    blocks = []\n    for i in range(num_blocks):\n        block = grid[i*block_size : (i+1)*block_size]\n        anti_sum = 0\n        for r in range(block_size):\n            anti_sum += block[r][block_size - 1 - r]   # column: 2\u2011r\n        blocks.append((anti_sum, block))\n    # sort by anti\u2011diagonal sum\n    blocks.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\n    # median index (odd number of blocks)\n    median_idx = num_blocks // 2\n    return blocks[median_idx][1]",
